1. Interactive
• Interactive: You type a command (like ls or mkdir), hit Enter, see the output, and get a prompt (like $ or user@computer:~#) waiting for your next command.
• Non-Interactive: This is when a machine runs a script automatically in the background. No human is typing, and no visual prompt is shown.

 Non-Login (vs. Login)

 • Login Shell: The very first shell that opens when you log into a computer. For example, when you boot up a Linux machine and sign in, or when you connect to a remote server using SSH. It loads profile files like ~/.bash_profile.
• Non-Login Shell: A shell opened after you are already logged into your user account

