What is .bashrc Used For?

The .bashrc file is used to store persistent terminal customizations. Without it, any changes you make during a terminal session (like creating a temporary shortcut) disappear the moment you close the window.

Its most common use cases include:

• Creating Aliases: You can create custom shortcuts for long or complex commands. For example, adding alias ll='ls -la' allows you to type ll instead of the full list-all command.
• Modifying Environment Variables: It is frequently used to alter the system behavior by updating variables, most notably expanding your $PATH variable so the system knows where to find newly installed software executables.
• Loading Shell Functions: You can write small, custom shell functions to automate repetitive command line workflows.


File Location	~/.bashrc (Expands to /home/username/.bashrc).
Shell Type	    Runs specifically for Interactive, Non-Login Shells (e.g., opening a new terminal window or tab on your
                desktop, or typing bash inside an active terminal).
Scope	        Per-User Configuration. Each user on a Linux system has their own independent .bashrc file. (System-wide 
                changes for all users are typically managed in /etc/bash.bashrc).

